# A thrown ball vs the textbook parabola — dynamic notebook
#
# A 0.145 kg baseball leaves [0, 1, 0] at v = [30, 30, 0] under uniform
# gravity [0, -9.81, 0] — the closed-form solution is x(t) = 30 t,
# y(t) = 1 + 30 t - 9.81 t²/2. Anchors: apex y ≈ 46.87 at t ≈ 3.06 s,
# range x = 183.6 by t = 6.12 s (grammar.md §9 Example 2 matches the
# simulated flight to ~1e-13). Watch the hud E (≈131.92, constant) and
# P (px constant at 4.35, py falling) as the parabola sweeps the window.
#
# Launch:   posim_notebook thrown_ball
#     or:   cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/thrown_ball.posim
# Press Start in the scene window (or type SCENE START here) to run
# and display the simulation.

new point { mass = 0.145, position = [0, 1, 0], velocity = [30, 30, 0] }
set system.gravity = [0, -9.81, 0]

# Baselines before any step: total energy, momentum, launch point
energy
momentum
get obj0.position

# Analytic anchors: range 30·6.12, apex time 30/9.81, apex height 1 + 30²/(2·9.81)
30 * 6.12
30 / 9.81
1 + 30 * 30 / (2 * 9.81)

scene create

# Playback: dt = 0.02 at ~30 ticks/s makes the 6.12 s flight take ~10 wall seconds
scene set_time_step 0.02

# Framing: the arc spans 183.6 x 46.9 units, centered near [92, 23].
# Move the look-at point to [90, 20, 0] and zoom 0.06 (distance 12 -> 200),
# which widens the default ±5..10-unit view to roughly ±100..165 units —
# the whole parabola fits with margin. Default yaw/pitch left alone.
scene translate 90 20
scene zoom 0.06
